## Preparation of ferritin-MOFs Timing $24-72h$
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL In this protocol, we use a variant of HuHF, $^{\mathrm{H122}}\mathrm{HuHF}$ for the formation of ferritin-MOFs. The protein can be expressed and purified, as described previously11. After purification, the protein is concentrated to $25\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (24meric cage), filtered through a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ filter, and stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for long-term storage in a buffered solution containing $50~\mathrm{mM}$ CHES $\mathrm{(pH}8.5\dot{)}$ , $150\mathrm{mMNaCl}$ Avoid using any buffers that would strongly chelate metal ions and inhibit protein–HA interactions. When considering alternative protein scaffolds, ensure that the protein is stable at $\mathrm{pH}8\mathrm{-}10$ at $200~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for protein-MOF self-assembly conditions (described below).
2 Warm a stock solution of $^{\mathrm{H122}}\mathrm{HuHF}$ ( $25~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ protein in a buffered solution containing $50~\mathrm{mM}$ CHES $\mathrm{(pH~}8.5)$ , $150\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mM~NaCl}}$ to RT prior to self-assembly experiments. Prepare a $10\mathrm{\mM}$ solution of the bidentate bridging linker ( $(p\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{h}$ or $m{\mathrm{-H}}_{2}{\mathrm{bdh}}$ ) in $50\ \mathrm{\mM}$ CHES $\mathrm{(pH~}9.5\mathrm{)}$ , $150\ \mathrm{mM\NaCl}$ . To determine the optimal conditions for growing high-quality crystals, the concentration of each component can be varied. As an example, here are variations presented for the components used to generate ferritin-MOFs: $1{-}12.5~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ of ferritin cage in a buffered solution containing $50~\mathrm{mM}$ CHES $\mathrm{(pH~}8.5\mathrm{)}$ ), $150\ \mathrm{mM}$ NaCl, $0.5{-}2\ \mathrm{mM}$ of the bidentate bridging linker, and 50–150 equiv. of a transition metal salt ( $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ ) per ferritin cage.   
4 Prepare the sitting drop and a reservoir solutions separately, and pipette into a 24-well Cryschem crystallization tray. See the table below for an example set of crystallization conditions to use for the formation of ferritin-MOFs.
CRITICAL STEP A thorough screen of commonly used precipitating and crowding agents is recommended to identify optimal conditions for the formation of diffraction-quality crystals; in our experience, low-molecular-weight PEGs (PEG 300, PEG 350 MME, and PEG 400) or pentaerythritol propoxylate (5/4 PO/OH) were the most successful. For initial screens, the reservoir solution can be supplemented with $0{-}20\%$ of a crystallization precipitant.
5 After preparing 24 screening solutions on a Cryschem Plate, cover the tray with clear packaging tape. Be sure to press down and flatten the tape across the plate such that the solutions cannot evaporate or mix with neighboring wells.
6 Inspect the crystal trays under a light microscope. After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , crystals should appear in the wells. Crystals are suitable for harvesting for structural analysis after $1{-}2\mathrm{~d~}$ .
## Characterization of ferritin-MOFs Timing 2–14 d
7 Analyze protein-MOF crystals using sc-XRD and/or SAXS measurements following the steps in options A and B respectively. For our work, sc-XRD data are collected at a synchrotron source (e.g., Stanford Synchrotron Radiation Laboratory or the Advanced Light Source at Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory) and analyzed using a suite of $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray crystallography programs85–88.
SAXS data are collected at a synchrotron source (e.g., Argonne National Laboratory-Advanced Photon Source) and analyzed using the powder diffraction processing software JADE (MDI). Minor adjustments for processing ferritin-MOFs can be found in ref. 17.
A) Structural analysis of ferritin-MOFs using sc-XRD $0$ Timing 1–7 d
(i) Briefly soak a single crystal in a cryoprotectant solution for 5–10 s using a mounted CryoLoop. For ferritin-MOFs, we have found success using a $50\%$ (wt/vol) solution of xylitol or PEP in $50~\mathrm{mM}$ CHES $\left(\mathrm{pH}\ 8.5-9.5\right)$ , $150\ \mathrm{mM\NaCl}$ .   
(ii) Rapidly plunge the crystals into liquid $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ . Transfer the CryoLoop into a magnetic CryoVial using a magnetic CrystalWand. CRITICAL STEP Once the crystals are frozen, they should be handled at liquid $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ temperatures. Do not allow the crystals to warm up to maintain suitable conditions for sc-XRD data collection.   
(iii) Collect data at a synchrotron facility. For ferritin-MOFs, data were collected at $100\mathrm{K}$ using 0.98 Å radiation.   
(iv) After data collection, process the collected images using a standard protein structural determination workflow. Briefly, integrate the collected images using iMosflm, and scale and merge the data using Aimless. Perform molecular replacement with Phaser using a previously solved structure as a search model. Perform rigid-body and further refinements in Phenix.
## (B) Structural analysis of ferritin-MOFs using SAXS ● Timing 1–7 d
(i) Prepare crystals for SAXS in 12-well cell culture plates. To gather enough crystals for SAXS measurements, each plate contains a single metal/linker combination. An example set of crystallization conditions to generate ferritin-MOFs for SAXS analysis is presented in the table below.
(ii) Crystals should form in $12{-}24\mathrm{h}$ . Harvest crystals after $^3\mathrm{d}$ , combining the crystals from all 24 wells into a $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ Eppendorf tube.
(iii) After the crystals are settled into the bottom of the tube, carefully pipette them into a $1.5~\mathrm{mm}$ quartz capillary tube with $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the reservoir solution from the tube. Seal the end of the capillary with modeling clay. Samples can be shipped to synchrotron facilities and stored under ambient temperature.
(iv) Collect data at a synchrotron facility. For ferritin-MOFs, data were collected with collimated X-rays (0.7293 Å, $17\mathrm{keV}$ ) with dimensions of $250\times250~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and exposure times between $0.5\mathrm{~s~}$ and $_\mathrm{~2~s~}$ . Scattered radiation was collected with a charge-coupled area detector, and 1D scattering data were obtained through an azimuthal averaging of 2D data to obtain plots of scattering intensity as a function of the scattering vector q: $q=4\pi\sin(\theta)/\lambda$ , where $\theta$ is $1/2$ of the scattering angle, and $\lambda$ is X-ray wavelength.
(v) Analyze data using the JADE processing software. Simulated powder diffraction modeling of the SAXS profiles can be generated in Mercury89.
