Steps 45–50, preparation of protein cages: 1–7 d   
Step 51A, AUC characterization of protein cages: $16{-}24\mathrm{~h~}$   
Step 51B, TEM characterization of protein cages: $^{1-2\mathrm{~h~}}$
## Procedure 2
Step 1A, preparation of $N^{1},N^{4}$ -dihydroxyterephthalamide: $18{-}24\mathrm{~h~}$ Step 1B, preparation of $N^{2},N^{3}$ -dihydroxyterephthalamide: 18–24 h Steps 2–6, preparation of ferritin-MOFs: 24–72 h Step 7A, structural analysis of ferritin-MOFs using sc-XRD: 1–7 d Step 7B, structural analysis of ferritin-MOFs using SAXS: 1–7 d
## Anticipated results
## HA-mediated protein cages
Site-selective conjugation of IHA to Cys-bearing proteins and subsequent purification by FPLC will yield pure protein conjugate, separated from any unmodified proteins during the FPLC NaCl gradient (Fig. $|5\mathrm{b},\mathrm{c}\rangle$ . Based on the quantities used in this protocol, we routinely obtain $30\mathrm{-}40\%$ yield for protein conjugates bearing two Cys residues and $50\mathrm{-}60\%$ yield for protein conjugates bearing a single Cys residue. The conjugation procedure described in this protocol can be repeated on a previously modified batch of proteins if overall yields are poor.
Incubation of both $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm n^{2+}$ ions with our cytochrome $c b_{562}$ yields discrete dodecameric and hexameric protein cages (Fig. 8a–c). AUC experiments are useful for solution characterization of the self-assembled particles and additionally serve to help identify conditions under which self-assembly occurs poorly (e.g., absence of both metal ions and using an impure HA-conjugated protein solution). Detailed procedures and characterization of HA-mediated protein cages can be found in ref. 10.
